---
source: crates/trust-cg-test/tests/cli.rs
expression: "help_of(&[\"fuzz\"])"
---
Planned differential-fuzzing dispatcher (WS3).

This command is not implemented in trust-cg 0.1.0. It exits 2 without generating programs, invoking
tools, filing issues, or writing a result artifact. Its options are reserved for a future release.

Usage: trust-cg-test fuzz [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --driver <DRIVER>
          Planned fuzz driver; ignored in v0.1.0

          Possible values:
          - csmith:     Planned csmith random C program generator
          - yarpgen:    Planned YARPGen random C/C++ program generator
          - trustirgen: Planned in-tree trust_ir random IR generator
          - all:        Planned selection of every driver

          [default: all]

  -f, --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (human, json, junit)

          Possible values:
          - human: Pretty table + narrative, targeted at humans
          - json:  Machine-readable JSON
          - junit: JUnit XML for CI consumers

          [default: human]

      --duration <DUR>
          Planned wall-clock duration; ignored in v0.1.0

          [default: 10m]

  -o, --out <PATH>
          Override the command-specific output file or directory.

          See the selected subcommand's help for its output type and default.

      --seeds <N>
          Planned seed cap; ignored in v0.1.0

      --timeout <SECS>
          Per-unit timeout in seconds. Subcommands may apply per-unit defaults when this is unset

      --optlevel <N>
          Planned optimization level; ignored in v0.1.0

          [default: 2]

      --parallel <N>
          Worker count (honors cargo-serialization lock). Default: auto

  -q, --quiet
          Suppress progress bars; errors still go to stderr

      --reduce
          Planned reduction control; no reducer runs in v0.1.0

  -v, --verbose...
          Show debug-level events. Repeat for more: -vv, -vvv

      --no-cache
          Ignore proof / corpus caches

      --dry-run
          Print what would run without executing external tools

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
